00:00:08.806 --> 00:00:10.430
DANA HOFFMAN: Hi,
I'm Dana, an engineer

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:13.010
at Google working on Google
Cloud Platform and open source

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:13.654
integrations.

00:00:13.654 --> 00:00:15.820
PADDY FORAN: And I'm Paddy,
an engineer at HashiCorp

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.209
on the Terraform team.

00:00:17.209 --> 00:00:19.000
Terraform is a tool
that lets you provision

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.470
your infrastructure
on any cloud,

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.270
or even across multiple clouds,
in a safe, easy to manage way.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:24.770
DANA HOFFMAN: In
the last two videos

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.080
you saw demos of Vagrant
development environments

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.090
with Google Compute
Engine, and Packer,

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.040
for producing Compute
Engine images.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.740
We're going to pick up where we
left off last time with using

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.150
Packer to create
a Compute Engine

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.460
image with our
80s-style arcade game,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.190
Hyperspace, pre-installed
and ready to go.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.890
Continuing our story, our
game has neared completion.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.880
And now we're getting
ready for our big launch.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:48.360
But because we know our
game is going to be viral,

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.850
we want to make sure that
we can handle the load.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.820
So rather than run Hyperspace
on a single compute instance,

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.117
and hope that it can handle
thousands of players,

00:00:56.117 --> 00:00:58.200
we're going to take advantage
of the architecture.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.390
And create one image with
the front end web server

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.916
and a separate image
with the back end server.

00:01:02.916 --> 00:01:04.290
And I had to do
for that was make

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.750
some customizations to my
Hyperspace install scripts.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.970
And use Packer to create
two images instead

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.890
of the single all-in-one image
you saw in the last video.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.269
So now that we have a scalable
way to package Hyperspace,

00:01:14.269 --> 00:01:15.810
let's talk about
how we can deploy it

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.880
to Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.464
Paddy, any idea how we
should go about that?

00:01:20.464 --> 00:01:22.880
PADDY FORAN: Actually Dana, I
think I know just the thing.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.005
Today we're going use
another HashiCorp tool called

00:01:25.005 --> 00:01:27.780
Terraform, to help us deploy
Hyperspace with this new split

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.352
front end and back end approach.

00:01:29.352 --> 00:01:30.810
Terraform is a tool
that allows you

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.226
to write declarative
configuration

00:01:32.226 --> 00:01:34.440
files describing the state
of your infrastructure.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.650
It allows to provision resources
such as VMs, disks, networking

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.935
configurations, and databases.

00:01:39.935 --> 00:01:41.560
And it works with
many Cloud providers,

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.170
including Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.754
But you already knew
that, right Dana?

00:01:44.754 --> 00:01:46.294
DANA HOFFMAN: Yeah,
you could say I'm

00:01:46.294 --> 00:01:47.730
pretty familiar with Terraform.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.063
I'm the lead engineer at
Google working on our Terraform

00:01:50.063 --> 00:01:52.260
integration with
Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.049
We've heard from plenty
of our customers,

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:55.590
that they really
like using Terraform

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.380
to manage GCP and appreciate
that Terraform has support

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.970
for multiple cloud providers.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:03.420
With Terraform, users can
manage GCP resources spanning

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.270
many of our products such
as Compute Engine, Cloud

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:10.100
Storage, IM, Cloud SQL,
CLOUD DNS, et cetera.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.200
But for this demo, we're going
to focus on Compute Engine.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.719
So let's get started.

00:02:14.719 --> 00:02:16.510
PADDY FORAN: The first
thing you want to do

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.460
is go to terraform.io
and download

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:19.999
the software for your platform.

00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:21.790
Refer back to the
Vagrant and Packer videos

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.712
for setting up your GCP project.

00:02:23.712 --> 00:02:26.170
For this demo we're going to
use Terraform from an existing

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:29.290
Google Compute Engine instance.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.210
Navigating to the
list of GC images,

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:34.090
you can see that I already
have two separate images.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.400
One for the front end
and one for the back.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.854
OK, before we step through
Terraform config file,

00:02:39.854 --> 00:02:41.520
let's show you how
to use Terraform plan

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.774
to see what resources
Terraform will create.

00:02:44.774 --> 00:02:46.940
In this case we don't have
any of our infrastructure

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:47.482
provisioned.

00:02:47.482 --> 00:02:49.690
And as you can see, Terraform
is expecting provisions

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.130
to set them new resources
with zero changes and zero

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:53.750
deletions.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.120
When we're happy with what
we expect Terraform to do,

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.220
we can then run Terraform
apply to perform

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.348
the actual infrastructure
provisioning.

00:03:03.595 --> 00:03:06.220
While that's provisioning let's
walk through that configuration

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:07.190
file.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:09.760
This first section of the
file contains the GCP project

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:10.644
parameters.

00:03:10.644 --> 00:03:13.060
I haven't specified a service
account key in this example,

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.460
because I'm running Terraform
on a Compute Engine instance.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.501
And Terraform is smart
enough to pull credentials

00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:19.570
from the metadata service.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:22.780
Next, you see I have a resource
specifying my back end GC

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.390
virtual machine instance,
including the name, zone

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:27.470
location, and machine type.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.240
Here is the snippet
where I've specified

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.180
that I want this instance
to use the back end

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.580
image built with Packer.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:36.670
Skipping down to the stanza
for our front end VM instances,

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.249
you can see that I've specified
the other package image.

00:03:39.249 --> 00:03:40.790
But we want three
of these instances.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:43.760
So we need to set
its count to three.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.790
I'm also provisioning
a GCU load balancer

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:47.929
using a target pool
and forwarding rule.

00:03:47.929 --> 00:03:50.470
Using the output parameter, I
can tell Terraform to print out

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:52.390
the load balancers
virtual IP address when

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.327
it's completed provisioning
all of our resources.

00:03:55.327 --> 00:03:57.410
DANA HOFFMAN: Flipping
back to the other terminal,

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.380
you can see that Terraform
has indeed finished

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.140
provisioning our resources.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.450
And here's the front end IP.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.310
Let's put that IP
address into our browser

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.269
and see what happens.

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:08.560
PADDY FORAN: It works, awesome.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:09.310
DANA HOFFMAN: Yay.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:10.324
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:10.324 --> 00:04:12.240
PADDY FORAN: OK well
let's go ahead and delete

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.680
all of these resources.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.700
We can easily do that
with Terraform destroy.

00:04:18.733 --> 00:04:20.399
DANA HOFFMAN: So that
